# build output
/bin/
/dist/
*.exe
# What a build leaves behind when it cannot replace a binary that is running:
# the old one is renamed out of the way rather than removed. It is the same
# twenty megabytes as the file above and just as much not source.
*.exe~
*.syso
# Resource objects are generated, and these two are kept all the same: they are
# what puts the program's icon in the executable, "go build" reads them without
# being told to, and a clone that had to run a tool before the icon appeared
# would be a clone whose icon nobody has. The exception stands after the rule it
# undoes, because the last line that matches is the one that decides.
!cmd/gserp/rsrc_windows_amd64.syso
!cmd/gserp/rsrc_windows_arm64.syso

# runtime data
/data/
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm
# A database put aside before something risky is still a database, and the rule
# above does not catch it: a copy is named after the day it was taken and no
# longer ends in .db.
*.db.bak*
# What the program writes when it is started as a service rather than from a
# console. It lands in the directory the service was started from, which on a
# developer machine is this one.
*.log

# local config and secrets
/config.yaml
/proxies.txt
*.local.yaml
# What the interface writes when somebody saves settings. It holds the key to
# the service, and it is written next to the program, which on a developer's
# machine is next to this file.
gserp-settings.json

# exports
/out/
*.xlsx
*.csv
*.jsonl

# editors
/.idea/
/.vscode/
*.swp

# internal working material — never published
# The contents are excluded rather than the directory itself, because git
# cannot take a file back out of a directory it has excluded whole — and one
# file in there is written for whoever calls this program rather than for
# whoever builds it.
/docs/*
!/docs/api.md
/.superpowers/
/testdata-corpus/
*.har
